module extdeps.git.inspect


data extdeps_external_authority_anchor: extdeps.external_authority.ExternalAuthority = extdeps.external_authority.ExternalAuthority {
  uri: extdeps.uri.Uri {
    scheme: extdeps.uri.Https
    locator: "git-scm.com/docs"
  }
}

data git_inspect_service_note: String = "Read-only repository-root, revision, and tree inspection operations are de-fused from the broad git.Core service so consumers pay only the upstream facts they inspect. Toplevel, HeadCommit, and ShowTree move here with their existing shapes; MergeBase and ListTreePathsAtRevision extend this same inspection authority for revision-addressed Rust-source observation. No second Git inspection surface remains in extdeps.git."

// `--no-optional-locks` IS WHAT MAKES `readonly` TRUE, AND ITS ABSENCE MADE THIS DECLARATION FALSE.
// Measured: running this operation's own argv without the flag against a repository CHANGES the index
// file (md5 before/after differ), because `git status` refreshes the index and writes cached stat
// information back. With the flag the index is byte-identical, and the reported entries are unchanged
// -- so the flag costs nothing and buys the property the operation already claimed. A `readonly`
// declaration that does not hold is worse than a missing one, because extdeps is the layer whose whole
// job is to model what upstream actually does, and every consumer downstream reasons from it as a fact.
//
// The sentence below said this operation pinned every flag the manual says a script must pin. It did
// not, and that is why the omission survived: the claim of completeness was the thing that stopped
// anyone rechecking. Corrected in place rather than appended to, so the false version does not stand
// beside the true one.
//
// THIS FLAG CANNOT BE WITNESSED, AND THAT IS THE SAME FUSION §3 NAMES. It lives inside a `transport
// shell` literal, and a literal in a service block cannot be called by a test -- which is exactly the
// defect `ssh.Session.ExecPortableWords` was built to correct, where one site required host-key
// verification and another disabled it, spelled as indistinguishable strings with no test able to name
// the disagreement. So nothing stops a later reader deleting this flag as noise; the protection is this
// paragraph and the measurement in it, which is strictly weaker than a witness and is recorded as such.
// It climbs when the argv is lifted out of the literal into a callable authority, the same lift the
// deployed-tree observation performs for its own words.
//
// STILL OPEN, NAMED RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE REDISCOVERED: `input {}` means this operation cannot name a
// repository at all, so it observes whatever directory the process happens to be standing in. That is
// the ambient-context class, one notch below an operation that at least takes a path, and closing it
// changes every call site -- a separate repair from this one.
//
// WorkingTreeStatus pins the flags Git's own manual says a script must pin. `--porcelain=v1` because the
// manual states plain `--porcelain` may change and that a version should be given for scripting, so the
// version is named rather than implied. `-z` because paths may contain spaces, quotes, or newlines and the
// non-NUL form C-quotes them, which would make every consumer reimplement Git's quoting rules to recover a
// filename. `--untracked-files=all` because the default `normal` collapses an untracked directory to the
// directory name alone, and a consumer asking "does this tree contain files HEAD does not name" needs the
// files, not a folded ancestor.
//
// The mock answers a CLEAN tree because the runner contract hands each job a fresh checkout; a mocked
// observation must report the state the contract guarantees rather than fabricate a dirty one.
//
// IgnoredFiles is a SECOND operation rather than an `--ignored=matching` flag on the first, and the reason is
// measured, not stylistic. `git status --porcelain=v1 -z --untracked-files=all --ignored=matching` reports a
// directory that itself matches an ignore pattern as one folded entry — `!! ign/` — and never names the file
// inside it. That is the same folded-ancestor loss `--untracked-files=all` exists to avoid, and it is fatal
// here specifically, because a consumer asking "which ignored files would the source sync copy" must filter
// each path against the deployed-scope authority and must name the offending path in its refusal; a folded
// directory can do neither. `git ls-files --others --ignored --exclude-standard -z` answers with real file
// paths (`ign/f.dag`), which is the granularity the question needs. Verified by execution against git 2.43.0
// on a constructed repository, both spellings side by side.
//
// `--exclude-standard` is pinned because without it the command applies no ignore rules at all and would
// return every untracked path — the opposite of the question. The output is a bare NUL-separated path list,
// so unlike porcelain v1 it carries no status codes and no rename-source continuation to track.
// MergeBase NAMES THE REPOSITORY IT INSPECTS, AND THE OPERAND IS REQUIRED.
// An ambient operation answers about whichever checkout the process happens to occupy, so the
// same call means different things depending on where it was invoked from -- and a wrong answer
// is INDISTINGUISHABLE from a right one, because two repositories can hold refs that look
// identical. An OPTIONAL operand would keep the ambient form writable, so it is required: the
// state this replaces has no spelling.
//
// THIS IS THE REPOSITORY'S OWN ESTABLISHED ANSWER, NOT A NEW CONVENTION. extdeps.git RemoteUrlIn
// already takes `repository_path` and renders `git -C {repository_path}`, for exactly this reason
// -- it could otherwise confirm the remote of the wrong checkout. This operation is brought into
// line with that authority rather than inventing a second way to say the same thing.
//
// WHY THE OPERAND SITS ON THE OPERATION AND NOT ON THE INVOCATION. "Which repository does this
// inspect" is a fact about the git operation's SUBJECT; "what directory did the OS start this
// process in" is a transport fact. Modeling the first as the second would push repository
// selection into every handler that runs a command -- the N x M adapter shape. One
// repository-addressed operation with one shell realization is not that.
//
// THE SIBLING POPULATION IS DECLARED RATHER THAN SILENTLY LEFT. Five operations in this service
// are still repository-ambient by the same mechanism: HeadCommit, WorkingTreeStatus,
// IgnoredFiles, ListTreePathsAtRevision and ShowTree. They are NOT ported here because each
// carries its own consumer census and this cut is scoped to the operation whose ambient
// repository blocked a wet observation. Toplevel is deliberately NOT in that list: its purpose IS
// to discover the ambient repository, so naming one would be circular. RUNG for the five:
// mitigatable, and the mitigation is only that production happens to run from the repository
// root. NEXT TRIGGER: the first consumer that must inspect a repository other than its own
// process position -- which is what forced this one.

// MergeBase CARRIES ITS EXIT CODE BECAUSE THE Bool COLLAPSES AN ANSWER WITH AN ABSENCE OF ONE.
// `git merge-base` exits 1 when the two commits genuinely share NO merge base -- unrelated
// histories, which is a FACT about the pair -- and 128 when the ref is invalid or this is not a
// repository, which is a failure to look. Both render `exit_success = false`, so a consumer
// holding only the Bool must either refuse both (losing a real answer) or admit both (treating an
// unreadable repository as proof that two revisions are unrelated). That operation's `exit` block
// has always DECLARED the distinction; its output simply never surfaced it, so the declaration was
// unreachable by any caller.

// ResolveRefCommit is a SEPARATE operation from ShowTree rather than a format flag on it, and the
// separation is the point: `%T` and `%H` answer different questions about the same ref. The tree is
// the CONTENT at that ref; the commit is its identity INCLUDING ancestry, which the tree does not
// carry -- two commits with byte-identical trees can have different parents and therefore different
// merge bases. A consumer that needs the merge to be reproducible needs this one, and a consumer
// that needs to compare content needs the other; collapsing them into one operation with a format
// argument would let a caller ask for content and be answered with identity.
service git.Inspect {
  operation Toplevel {
    input {}
    output { path: std.types.FilePath from "stdout" }
    readonly
    transport shell { argv: ["git", "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"] }
    exit {
      0 => Unit
      128 => String "Not a git repository"
    }
    mock_response {
      0 => { path: "." } "hermetic checkout root: the runner contract binds cwd = repo toplevel, so the relative root composes with checkout-rooted reads exactly as the absolute toplevel does wet"
    }
  }

  operation HeadCommit {
    input {}
    output {
      sha: std.types.CommitSha from "stdout"
      success: Bool from "exit_success"
    }
    readonly
    transport shell { argv: ["git", "rev-parse", "HEAD"] }
    exit {
      0 => Unit
      128 => String "Not a git repository"
    }
  }

  operation WorkingTreeStatus {
    input {}
    output {
      entries_nul: String from "stdout"
      success: Bool from "exit_success"
      stderr: String from "stderr"
    }
    readonly
    transport shell { argv: ["git", "--no-optional-locks", "status", "--porcelain=v1", "-z", "--untracked-files=all"] }
    exit {
      0 => Unit
      128 => String "Not a git repository"
    }
    mock_response {
      0 => { entries_nul: "", success: true, stderr: "" } "hermetic checkout is clean: the runner contract hands the job a fresh checkout, so the empty entry list is the state a mocked observation must report rather than a fabricated dirty one"
    }
  }

  operation IgnoredFiles {
    input {}
    output {
      paths_nul: String from "stdout"
      success: Bool from "exit_success"
      stderr: String from "stderr"
    }
    readonly
    transport shell { argv: ["git", "ls-files", "--others", "--ignored", "--exclude-standard", "-z"] }
    exit {
      0 => Unit
      128 => String "Not a git repository"
    }
    mock_response {
      0 => { paths_nul: "", success: true, stderr: "" } "hermetic checkout carries no ignored files: the runner contract hands the job a fresh checkout, so the empty path list is the state a mocked observation must report rather than a fabricated one"
    }
  }

  operation MergeBase {
    input { repository_path: String, left: std.types.GitRef, right: std.types.GitRef }
    output {
      sha: std.types.CommitSha from "stdout"
      success: Bool from "exit_success"
      exit_code: Int from "exit_code"
      stderr: String from "stderr"
    }
    readonly
    transport shell { argv: ["git", "-C", "{repository_path}", "merge-base", "{left}", "{right}"] }
    exit {
      0 => Unit
      1 => String "No merge base"
      128 => String "Invalid ref or not a git repository"
    }
  }

  operation ListTreePathsAtRevision {
    input { ref: std.types.GitRef }
    output {
      paths_nul: String from "stdout"
      success: Bool from "exit_success"
      stderr: String from "stderr"
    }
    readonly
    transport shell { argv: ["git", "ls-tree", "-rz", "--name-only", "{ref}"] }
    exit {
      0 => Unit
      128 => String "Invalid ref or not a git repository"
    }
  }

  operation ResolveRefCommit {
    input { ref: std.types.GitRef }
    output {
      sha: std.types.CommitSha from "stdout"
      success: Bool from "exit_success"
      stderr: String from "stderr"
    }
    readonly
    transport shell { argv: ["git", "show", "-s", "--format=%H", "{ref}"] }
    exit {
      0 => Unit
      128 => String "Invalid ref or not a git repository"
    }
  }

  operation ShowTree {
    input { ref: std.types.GitRef }
    output {
      tree: String from "stdout"
      success: Bool from "exit_success"
      stderr: String from "stderr"
    }
    readonly
    transport shell { argv: ["git", "show", "-s", "--format=%T", "{ref}"] }
    exit {
      0 => Unit
      128 => String "Invalid ref or not a git repository"
    }
  }
}

// MERGE-BASE EXIT SEMANTICS ARE A FACT OF GIT, SO THEY ARE MODELED HERE BESIDE THE EXIT TABLE THAT
// DECLARES THEM, and not re-derived by each consumer as bare integers compared with `==`. The
// operation above already states the contract -- 0, 1, 128 with distinct meanings -- and a consumer
// that mints its own `merge_base_exit_ok: Int = 0` beside it is holding a second representation of
// this module's fact: the §2/§3 undeclared sum, free to drift from the exit table the day a fourth
// code is declared, and free to be compared against the wrong operation's codes with no complaint
// from anything.
//
// THE THREE OUTCOMES ARE NOT A RANKING AND THE MIDDLE ONE IS NOT AN ERROR. Exit 1 is a legitimate
// ANSWER -- git looked and there is no common ancestor -- which is precisely what lets a consumer
// distinguish "these histories never met" from "I could not find out", the distinction §5 forbids
// collapsing. Exit 128 is the refusal: a bad object or a path that is not a repository, where
// nothing about the two revisions has been established. Rendering 128 as NoCommonAncestor would
// read an unreadable repository as proof that two revisions are unrelated.
//
// THE FIELD IS `base_hex`, NOT `stdout`, AND THAT RENAME IS THE ACTUAL FIX. It never held raw
// stdout: the operation's output binding delivers `sha` already stripped of git's line terminator,
// so a field named for the transport was describing something the transport does not hand over.
//
// THE NEWLINE CLAIM THIS PARAGRAPH ORIGINALLY MADE WAS FALSE AND IS CORRECTED BY MEASUREMENT.
// Review 54424 reported that a future caller passing raw stdout would get an "undecodable" refusal
// on a trailing newline, and I wrote a paragraph here asserting exactly that mechanism -- citing
// `git merge-base` emitting a newline after the fortieth hex digit, which it does. What I had not
// checked was whether that byte survives to this fold. It does not. Receipt: a recorded fixture's
// stored `sha` is 40 characters with no terminator, and REMOVING the trim below leaves the whole
// wet matrix green -- a mutation that changes nothing is the proof the guard was inert. The
// caller's trim, which this change deleted, was equally inert for the same reason.
//
// So the trim stays as idempotent normalization at the boundary that owns the wire format, and it
// is described as what it is: a total function over any string a caller hands this fold, NOT a
// live defect being closed. Calling it the latter would have been a fabricated mechanism sitting
// in a paragraph justifying its own code -- the same class as an inflated rung, and the reason the
// measurement is recorded here rather than the conclusion alone.
//
// Decoding that hex into an object id still belongs to the consumer that owns the id type; this
// module owns what git SAID and what its exit code MEANS, not how the caller names commits.
type MergeBaseOutcome
  = MergeBaseReported { base_hex: String }
  | NoCommonAncestor
  | MergeBaseRefused { exit_code: Int, stderr: String }

data merge_base_exit_reported: Int = 0

data merge_base_exit_no_common_ancestor: Int = 1

// Total over every integer, deliberately: an undeclared exit code is a REFUSAL carrying the code
// that produced it, never a fourth silent arm and never a default that resolves to an answer.
fn merge_base_outcome(exit_code: Int, stdout: String, stderr: String) -> MergeBaseOutcome {
  if exit_code == merge_base_exit_reported {
    MergeBaseReported { base_hex: std.algebra.trim(s: stdout) }
  } else {
    if exit_code == merge_base_exit_no_common_ancestor {
      NoCommonAncestor
    } else {
      MergeBaseRefused { exit_code: exit_code, stderr: stderr }
    }
  }
}
